  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "tdu+g8EQOB6WGJ7Rp6K7EXuw3AA=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:39:31.134Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "v6.0.0 opens its Breaking Changes section with the runtime floor:\n\n> **Node.js 24 is now the minimum supported version.**\n\n```bash\nnode --version          # must be >= 24.0.0\nnvm install 24 && nvm use 24\n```\n\nIt is a hard requirement, not a recommendation, and it is the first thing to check when a v6 project fails in an unexplained way during install or codegen. The tutorial track agrees: the Manual Todo tutorial's prerequisites list \"Node.js 24 and a package manager (pnpm or npm) installed\" rather than any earlier range.\n\nWorth pairing this with the other toolchain shifts in the same release, because they land together and a stale Node is often not the only thing lagging: `tsdown` replaced the previous build toolchain across `analytics-engine`, `builder-tools`, `ph-cli`, `ph-cmd`, `registry`, `shared` and `design-system`; processors now publish distinct `node` and `browser` entrypoints; and `sideEffects: false` was added to generated package boilerplate with pglite, jszip and renown crypto lazy-loaded.\n\nAlso from the same release: `ph-cmd` now delegates `init` and all forwarded commands to the versioned `ph-cli` binary, so version pinning finally works correctly \u2014 which means \"which Node am I on\" and \"which ph-cli am I actually running\" are now two separate questions worth asking independently.\n\nProvenance: academy `ReleaseNotes/v6.0.0.md:182` (Breaking Changes) and `03-Build/01-ManualTodoTutorial/01-CreateNewPowerhouseProject.md:24` (Prerequisites), academy 6.2.2-dev.45.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T14:39:30.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:39:31.134Z"
    }
  },
